1:# Win the answer tasks, then prove parity
3:LFM3 can plausibly match GUI-Owl-1.5-2B on this budget, but only against the right target and only by fixing one behaviour: answering questions. The fair target is GUI-Owl under our on-device budget (one screenshot, 4-bit weights, llama.cpp), where it scores **23/116**. Its self-reported **67.9** comes from a five-screenshot bf16 harness that costs 61 s per step on our Mac, and nothing reachable here can claim or beat that number. Against the matched number, the whole gap sits in AndroidWorld's **25 answer-scored tasks**: GUI-Owl answered **10** correctly, while LFM3 has never emitted an `answer` action and instead declares "complete" after navigating. On the other 91 tasks v1 already solves **15 to GUI-Owl's 13**. Published small-agent results and our own v2/v3 runs agree that the content of supervision (thoughts, answer-terminated trajectories, recovery data) and environment RL move AndroidWorld, while more static demonstrations do not. The next GPU hour should therefore train v4: v3 plus navigate-then-answer episodes in AndroidWorld's answer formats, with hyperparameters and image budget unchanged. Proving parity costs more than reaching it. A single 116-task run detects only gaps of about 14 tasks, and seed 30 has become a development set. A defensible "matches" therefore needs a pre-registered 8-task non-inferiority margin, at least three sealed held-out seeds (about 350 paired episodes), template-clustered tests, and GUI-Owl's best one-screenshot configuration as the comparator. All of this is paid for in emulator hours, not GPU hours.
5:## GUI-Owl's matched lead is ten answered questions
7:The comparison target has to be fixed before the method. GUI-Owl-1.5-2B reports **67.9% on AndroidWorld (AW)** using its authors' harness. That harness keeps the last five screenshots as multi-turn image messages, along with the model's own replies. It sends full-resolution PNGs to a bf16 vLLM server: up to about 3.07 MP, or roughly 2,500 visual tokens per 1080×2400 screen. It also forces a `terminate` on the step after an `answer` ([gui_owl.py](https://github.com/X-PLUG/MobileAgent/blob/main/Mobile-Agent-v3.5/android_world_v3.5/android_world/agents/gui_owl.py); [HF card](https://huggingface.co/mPLUG/GUI-Owl-1.5-2B-Instruct)). On our Mac that configuration costs **61 s per step at about 10.7k prompt tokens**, against 2.25–2.7 s for LFM3 ([GUI-Owl lessons](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/docs/GUI_OWL_LESSONS.md)).
9:The number we can actually compare against comes from running GUI-Owl under our deployment budget: its official prompt and action parser, one screenshot capped at about 1,024 visual tokens, Q4_K_M weights with a Q8 mmproj in llama.cpp, temperature 0, and AW's default step budget of 10× task complexity at seed 30. Under those settings it scores **23/116 (19.8%)** ([summary](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/androidworld/guiowl15_2b_matched.summary.json)). Precision is matched, because LFM3's AW runs use the same Q4_K_M plus Q8_0-mmproj artifacts ([eval protocol](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/docs/EVAL_PROTOCOL.md)). If anything, GUI-Owl got the richer input: up to 1,024 visual tokens against LFM3's ~720, and 2,261 mean prompt tokens against 1,145.
11:The 48-point drop from 67.9 is a configuration loss, not a measurement bug. Our step budget is the official one, and faithful third-party reruns of the 32B sibling land within half a point of its published score (**69.3 ± 0.5** over three seeds) ([Beyond the GUI Paradigm](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.19388)). The authors' own ablation attributes only about 2–3 points to history images, measured on GUI-Owl-7B on OSWorld ([Mobile-Agent-v3](https://arxiv.org/html/2508.15144v2)), whereas serving-stack differences alone cost GUI-Owl-7B 30–45 AW points in user reports, where the maintainer's first question was whether the user had quantized the model ([issue #203](https://github.com/X-PLUG/MobileAgent/issues/203)). This collapse under on-device budgets is general, not specific to GUI-Owl. MAI-UI-2B reports 49.1 in its own harness, but on a phone NPU at Q4_0 with 512×512 input it scored **8.2% on AW's easy split and 0% on medium plus hard** ([DroidPrefill](https://arxiv.org/html/2611.20772)). The matched comparison is therefore the product-relevant one. It must, however, carry the label "GUI-Owl-1.5-2B under a one-screenshot, 4-bit budget."
13:Within the matched run, the gap is concentrated almost entirely in one task type. In our AW copy, **25 of the 116 tasks are scored on the agent's answer text**, which the information-retrieval checkers read from `interaction_cache` ([information_retrieval.py](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/android_world/android_world/task_evals/information_retrieval/information_retrieval.py)). GUI-SD-v2 counts 27 under a broader definition ([GUI-SD-v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.27307)). The table splits the seed-30 results by task type and by how each episode ended ([GUI-Owl failure modes](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/androidworld/guiowl15_2b_matched.failure_modes.json); [v1 failure modes](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/androidworld/lfm3vl2b_ft.failure_modes.json)).
17:| Answer-scored tasks solved (of 25) | **10** (5 Simple Calendar, 3 Joplin counts, 2 Tasks) | **0** |
18:| Action tasks solved (of 91) | 13 | **15** |
20:| Episodes the agent ended itself | 24 (13 `terminate`, 11 `answer`) | 60 (`status complete`) |
22:| Wrong terminal claims | 1 wrong answer, 0 false completes | 45 false completes |
26:On action tasks the two models are level: 8 tasks were solved only by v1 and 6 only by GUI-Owl, a difference within noise. On answer tasks the gap is complete, and it is behavioural. GUI-Owl solved 10 answer tasks; on 8 of those, v1 navigated for 3–8 plausible steps and then emitted `status complete`. In SimpleCalendarEventsOnDate it opened Simple Calendar, tapped October 24 and declared completion. In NotesMeetingAttendeeCount it searched for the meeting title, opened the result and stopped ([v1 trajectories](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/androidworld/lfm3vl2b_ft/run_20261117T030335477504)).
28:v1 learned AndroidControl's habit that every episode ends in `complete`. AndroidControl's action space has no `answer` or `terminate` ([AndroidControl* card](https://huggingface.co/datasets/UI-MOPD/AndroidControl-Star)). Our prompt describes `answer` as a "reply to a question about the screen" ([agent_format.py](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/train/agent_format.py)), which fits single-screen QA rows but not a goal that requires navigating first and answering last.
30:AW's scoring rule explains why GUI-Owl's caution pays only on answer tasks. AW scores an episode `task_successful if done else 0` ([suite_utils.py](https://github.com/google-research/android_world/blob/main/android_world/suite_utils.py)), so a false `complete` and an exhausted budget both score zero. GUI-Owl's calibrated termination therefore earns points only where it ends with a correct answer or a correct `terminate`. On action tasks, LFM3's 45 false completes cost it nothing that GUI-Owl's 92 budget exhaustions did not also cost GUI-Owl. To match GUI-Owl under this budget, LFM3 does not need to navigate better. It needs to end question goals with a correct, correctly formatted answer.
44:AW did not move with them. Of the first 72 v3 episodes, 8 solved; paired with v1 on 70 tasks, each model won 2 tasks the other lost (p = 1.0). v3 also produced zero answer actions. A recheck at 104 finished episodes found 12 successes and still no answer action, and **12 of its 19 finished answer-scored episodes ended in `status complete`** ([v3 run](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/androidworld/lfm3vl2b_v3/run_20261117T210853476910)).
46:Two lessons follow from v3. First, ScreenQA's single-screen answers did not transfer to answering after navigation. Those answers also drifted toward full sentences (ScreenQA .724 against v2's .764, paired p = 0.008), the opposite of the bare values AW's checkers want. Second, v3 regressed on app names: on the 14 Markor tasks it called `open_app("Marker")` 28 times and "Markor" only 3 times, where v1 used "Markor" 11 times and "Marker" 4 times.
48:Inference-only fixes did not help either: runtime feedback notes plus lexical app-name resolution scored 10/116 on v1, not significantly different from 15. These are the three published lessons reproduced at our scale. Blank thoughts are harmful, more static demonstrations raise offline metrics but not online success, and harness annotations the model never trained on do not help.
54:GUI-Owl-1.5 is a full post-train of Qwen3-VL in three stages ([Mobile-Agent-v3.5](https://arxiv.org/html/2602.16855v1)). Continued pre-training on UI, knowledge, world-modelling and tool data comes first. SFT follows, on trajectories from a "hybrid data flywheel": human-authored task graphs per app, cloud-phone rollouts truncated at the last verified checkpoint, human demonstrations for tasks that still failed, and web-rendered "vibe-coded" virtual environments, with every step carrying a teacher-written thought that fuses observation, memory, reflection and progress. The last stage is MRPO, an online-RL method in the GRPO family that oversamples rollouts and swaps in opposite outcomes so groups never collapse to all-success or all-failure, training on one device family at a time. The ablations that exist are all at 7–8B. The unified thoughts are worth **+6.6 AW points** (65.0 → 71.6 at 8B-Thinking). In the v1 report, offline reasoning synthesis added about 18 points and three rounds of online rejection sampling about 16 ([Mobile-Agent-v3](https://arxiv.org/html/2508.15144v2)). Virtual-environment trajectories moved the in-house mobile evaluation from **50.0% to 86.7%**.
58:Every ≤4B agent above roughly 25% on AW combined three things: far more agent data than ours, explicit thought supervision, and either environment-collected trajectories or RL. The table summarizes them. Each AW number comes from the authors' own harness, so rows should not be ranked against each other.
64:| MAI-UI-2B ([paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2512.22047)) | Qwen3-VL-2B | self-evolving SFT + online GRPO | current + 2 previous screenshots | 49.1 | SFT 45.1 → RL 49.1 |
67:| Ferret-UI Lite-3B ([paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.26539)) | Apple internal 3B | SFT + 17K synthetic trajectories + RLVR | screenshot + action history | 28.0 (5-run mean) | no CoT 13.7 → long CoT 19.6 → +synthetic 25.2 → RL 28.0 |
68:| GUI-Libra-3B ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22190)) | Qwen2.5-VL-3B | SFT on 81K AC/Odyssey/AMEX steps re-annotated with reasoning → offline GRPO | screenshot + action history | 25.2 | SFT 5.2 → mixed 11.3 → reweighted 13.0 → GRPO 25.2 |
76:Several headline numbers do not compare cleanly with a strict no-AW rule. UI-Voyager trains on AW's own templates, AutoPlay generates its tasks on the 20 AW apps ([AutoPlay](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.25047)), and ADMIRE runs RL inside the AW sandbox ([ADMIRE](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.11524)). MAI-UI's prompt hard-codes AW app names ([prompt.py](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Tongyi-MAI/MAI-UI/main/MAI-UI/src/prompt.py)), and Step-GUI-4B's headline 75.8 is pass@3, against a pass@1 of 63.9 ([Step-GUI](https://arxiv.org/html/2512.15431)). Same-app training inflates AW, too. In AndroidWorld-Generalization, RL gains shrink from +26.1 on unseen instances to +15.7 on unseen templates and +8.3 on unseen apps ([AW-Generalization](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.07432)). Our rule forbids AW tasks, AW variants, AW evaluation trajectories and AW checkers as rewards. That is stricter than most of the field and stricter than anything GUI-Owl discloses.
80:### Thoughts, answers and recovery data have the largest affordable effects
82:Thoughts must be present in training. GUI-Libra-3B trained with CoT scored **13.0 against 5.2 without it**, and **25.2 against 12.2** after RL ([GUI-Libra](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22190)). MIRAGE-4B's action-only SFT fell below its own untuned base ([MIRAGE](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.04627)), and our v2 collapse to 3/116 shows the same effect at 2B.
84:Richer thoughts help, but they cost tokens and can cost grounding. Ferret-UI Lite-3B gained 2.1 points from short CoT and another 3.8 from long CoT ([Ferret-UI Lite](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.26539)). GUI-Libra-3B, however, grounded at 73.4 on ScreenSpot-v2 when its steps carried roughly 220 reasoning tokens, against 81.0 in a short no-reasoning mode, until RL closed the gap. The structured fields that best fit our failure modes are memory and progress. Privileged memory guidance raised GUI-SD-v2's information-retrieval success from **12/27 to 15/27** at 8B ([GUI-SD-v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.27307)).
90:Environment-grounded trajectories help when they are diverse and verified. Ferret's 0 → 5K → 13K → 17K judge-filtered rollouts took AW from 19.6 to 20.3, 22.4 and 25.2. Collecting such rollouts needs emulator time and programmatic checks on apps outside AW.
96:Offline GRPO with a small KL penalty is the strongest offline lever. It took GUI-Libra-3B **from 13.0 to 25.2 at KL 1e-3**, against 21.7 at KL 0 and 20.0 at KL 5e-2. The result carries three caveats: it started from a weak long-CoT SFT model, it used 81K curated steps, and Ferret gained only about 3 points from RL. No ≤4B paper reports LoRA-based GRPO on AW.
98:Token-level on-policy distillation is closed to us. It helped LiteGUI-2B on desktop (OSWorld 6.04 → 9.32, where plain SFT dropped it to 3.25) ([LiteGUI](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07505)). It needs a teacher that shares the student's tokenizer, though, and LFM3-VL has no larger sibling. Only self-distillation or sequence-level teacher data remain available.
113:No ≤4B paper ablates any of these axes on AW. The nearest evidence is from a robotics VLA, where LoRA r32 with a fully trained vision encoder matched full fine-tuning and freezing the vision side hurt ([LoRA for VLA](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.10172)). Under-training is not obviously harmful if RL follows. Ferret's RL gains were largest after short SFT, and in WinDOM an under-saturated SFT model gave GRPO a better starting point ([WinDOM](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.25964)).
117:### Hold the image budget, change the text and the answers
121:Carried text state is the input change that fits the budget: explicit state tracking with a single image moved GMA from 43.3 to 53.7. Resolution is a later sweep, not a v4 change. LFM3's 774,400-pixel cap (576×1280, about 720 tokens) sits between MAI-UI's "720p ≈ 1080p" and "540p substantially degrades" settings. Coordinate format is not a lever: point and box outputs differ by at most 1.3 click-accuracy points ([Phi-Ground](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.23779)), and our 0–1000 grid already matches GUI-Owl's.
123:What matters is answer design: the answer must end the episode, give bare values when the goal specifies a format (AW goals say, for example, "Express your answer as just a single number"), and use app names that exist on the device.
125:Inference tricks are harness variables, not model gains. Self-ranking three sampled actions by the actor's own log-probabilities raised AndroidControl-High exact match from 62.3 to 64.8 at 7B, offline ([GUI-Shepherd](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.23738)). A completion audit cut false completions from 8.0% to 5.4% without raising success ([Coati](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02644)). Either belongs in its own column of results or must be given to both arms.
133:| Navigate-then-answer trajectories, terse formats | our 0/25 vs GUI-Owl 10/25; GUI-SD-v2 memory guidance | IR 12/27 → 15/27 (8B) | +4 to +10 tasks | teacher labels + one SFT run | **v4 core** |
135:| Short memory/progress thoughts | GUI-Owl-1.5-8B; Ferret-3B | 65.0 → 71.6; 15.8 → 19.6 | +1 to +4 | teacher labels on answer episodes | v4, short form |
138:| Synthetic trajectories on clean apps | Ferret-3B | 19.6 → 25.2 at 17K | +2 to +5 | emulator nights + checkers | run-2 option |
139:| Offline GRPO, KL 1e-3 | GUI-Libra-3B; Ferret-3B | 13.0 → 25.2; ~25 → 28 | 0 to +5 | new tooling | only with confirmed Modal and a full smoke test |
150:A fair claim needs three arms on one pinned AW commit, AVD and seed list. The matched one-screenshot GUI-Owl arm already run is the fair comparator. LFM3 runs under exactly the same rules. A GUI-Owl native-configuration reference is always shown beside the matched number, never in place of it. One screenshot is not alien to GUI-Owl. Its v1 harness used one screenshot plus text conclusions ([Mobile-Agent-v3](https://arxiv.org/html/2508.15144v2)), and GUI-Owl-1.5's implementation in the MobileWorld repository defaults to `history_n: 1` ([MobileWorld gui_owl_1_5.py](https://github.com/Tongyi-MAI/MobileWorld/blob/main/src/mobile_world/agents/implementations/gui_owl_1_5.py)). Fairness still requires equal tuning effort: try that MobileWorld-style prompt on the development seed and keep whichever variant scores better. Every input change made for LFM3, such as an installed-app list, more images or more pixels, must be mirrored in the GUI-Owl arm.
154:The native reference can run locally or on Modal. Locally, at about 61 s per step, a 50-task stratified subset (all 25 answer tasks plus 25 random action tasks) at 8- or 16-bit takes about one night. If Modal credit is confirmed, a bf16 vLLM endpoint on Modal driven by the local emulators reproduces the official stack; a maintainer measures 1.2–1.5 s per inference on an A100 ([issue #252](https://github.com/X-PLUG/MobileAgent/issues/252)), so I estimate a full native pass at roughly 1.5–3 GPU-hours, mostly waiting on the emulator.
156:Every arm must record the same pinned protocol ([eval protocol](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/docs/EVAL_PROTOCOL.md)): AW commit 55ddaf5 with our local patches, a Pixel 6 API 33 **arm64** image (most papers use x86_64, and the effect of CPU architecture is unmeasured), the 10× task-complexity step budget with the done-flag scoring rule, temperature 0 with a one-retry parse policy, and the emulator ID for each episode. Exceptions should be scored two ways. The primary score counts them as failures over 116. The secondary score follows official AW code, which turns an exception into NaN and drops the episode, scoring over completed episodes only; by that rule v1 is 15/114 ([suite_utils.py](https://github.com/google-research/android_world/blob/main/android_world/suite_utils.py)). Infrastructure crashes are re-run under a rule written down in advance.
172:| "No significant difference" | seed 30 (done) | 116 | done |
188:## Spend the GPU on answering and the emulators on proof
192:v3 trained 1,250 steps of 32 rows (0.51 epoch of 78,952 rows) in **4,400 s on a Modal A100-40GB**, peaking at 11 GB of memory ([v3 training summary](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/training/lfm3vl2b_v3/train_summary.json)). With AndroidControl and development evaluations added, one v3-sized cycle takes about 1.5 GPU-hours, most of the ~2.5 G4-hours left on Colab. With $18.44 already spent on Modal this month, roughly one more run remains there if the earlier $26–28 ceiling still applies; treat that as unconfirmed. Emulator hours are free but slow, and they are the real constraint on fair evaluation. The plan below keeps hyperparameters, image budget and harness fixed for the training run, so that any change in AW can be attributed to data. Under the project's preflight rule, every paid step first runs end-to-end on a tiny limit, upload included.
198:| 2 | Train v4 with v3's exact hyperparameters; AndroidControl test, development splits and probes | ~1.5 G4-h | — | answer exact match on held-out question episodes clearly above 0; false-answer rate on action goals ≤ a few %; AndroidControl within ~2 points of v3 |
199:| 3 | AW seed-30 development pass, paired with v1, v3 and matched GUI-Owl (exploratory) | 0 | ~3.3 h | ≥5 answer-task successes and no action-task loss beyond noise |
205:The core of v4 is navigate-then-answer episodes. Take AndroidControl, GUI-Odyssey and AMEX *train* episodes on non-AW apps whose final screen shows readable facts such as dates, counts, names or times. Have a teacher VLM write a question answerable from that final screen, with an AW-style format instruction ("Express your answer as just a single number", "titles only, comma separated") and a terse answer; Qwen3-VL-8B, which already filled v3's intents, can serve ([vm_v3_intents.py](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/data/vm_v3_intents.py)). Keep the navigation prefix under the question goal, end with `answer(...)` and no trailing `status`, and deduplicate the questions against the 25 answer templates. Roughly 3–5K such episodes, about a tenth of sampled rows, is a reasonable size, though that is my estimate rather than a published recipe.
207:Two further sources and fixes feed the same goal. Codex found **132 structurally valid answer endpoints** in GUI-Libra's AMEX re-annotation; these can seed and validate the generator but cannot replace it ([Codex v4 audit](/Users/alpha/lfm3-android/results/codex/dataset_viability_v4.md)). ScreenQA answers should also be shortened to match AW's checkers. Finally, the prompt's `answer` description should be rewritten, in both training and inference, to present `answer` as the final reply whenever the goal asks a question.
209:Four smaller changes ride along. The final steps of answer episodes get a brief "seen: … / progress: …" thought stating the fact being reported, so the history carries it forward; this is the small-model form of GUI-Owl's memory-and-progress thoughts, and restricting it to answer episodes keeps teacher cost and decode latency low. Completion boundaries become explicit: question goals never end in `complete`, and action goals end in `complete` only on the gold final screen. The device's installed-app list goes into the prompt in training (the episode's app plus realistic distractors) and at inference to fix "Marker", with the same list given to GUI-Owl. No-change recovery rows, built from our own AndroidControl rows the GUI-Reflection way ([GUI-Reflection](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.08012)), go in at VeriGUI's 30% share only if v3's finished run shows loop counts near v2's rather than v1's, since a smaller share did nothing.
211:The second run depends on what step 3 reveals. If answers are emitted but mis-formatted, or the model answers on action goals, spend it on a corrected v4.1. If failures look like compounding errors, spend it on the earlier plan's on-policy correction round: roll v4 through about 1,500 AndroidControl and GUI-Odyssey train episodes on the Mac with its own history, and record first-divergence states with the gold label. At 7B, training on own-history states moved AW from 21.7 to 34.0 ([UI-S1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11543)), and keeping learner errors in the history beat pure expert distillation, 48.3 to 44.8 ([OpenMobile](https://arxiv.org/html/2604.15093)). Offline GRPO waits for a confirmed Modal budget and a pipeline that has already run end-to-end. Equally clear is what not to do: add more static human demonstrations, add history screenshots for parity, merge adapters, change the step budget or headline pass@k, fold harness tricks into model claims, or train the headline model on AW-app synthetic sets.
213:The task split bounds the outcomes; these are inferences from 3–8B effect sizes and our own split, not measurements. Assume action tasks hold at GUI-Owl's level, which v1 and v3 already reach. If v4 then turns half of the ten GUI-Owl-solved questions into correct, well-formatted answers, a single-seed score near **19–21/116** follows, roughly the level the three-seed non-inferiority test needs. Matching GUI-Owl's 10/25 answer rate gives **23–25**, which makes "matches within 8 tasks" likely. A significant win needs a 5–6 task lead per seed. That probably requires environment data or RL this budget cannot buy, unless LFM3 also solves answer tasks GUI-Owl misses, such as the six OpenTracks activity questions GUI-Owl never answered.
217:Splitting the matched run by task type changed the question. It is not whether a 2B LoRA model can navigate like a heavily RL-trained Qwen3-VL derivative under a phone budget; on action tasks it already does. The real question is whether LFM3 knows that a question ends with an answer. That makes this a rare case where the cheapest intervention is also the most targeted. It also means a successful v4 would establish something narrower than "LFM3 matches GUI-Owl": under the matched budget, the gap was a supervision gap, and published small-agent numbers do not survive on-device budgets for either model. Saying both plainly is part of making the claim correctly.
